>>merging branches
Merging is about bringing changes from one branch to another.
In Git we have two types of merges :
>Fast-Forward Merges (If branches have not diverged)
>3-Way Merges (if branches have diverged)

>>fast forward merges
This one is easy as branch that you are trying to merge is usually ahead and there are no conflicts.
When you are done working on a branch, you can merge it back into the main branch.

git checkout <branchname>
git merge <branchname>

git checkout main - This command switches to the main branch.
git merge red - This command merges the red branch into the main branch

>> 3-way merge
In this type of merge, the main branch has additional commits that are not present in the red branch. This is not a fast-forward merge. Here git looks at 3 different commits [common ancestor of branches + tips of each branch] and combines the changes into one merge commit.

>>managing conflicts
there are no shortcuts to resolve conflicts. You have to manually resolve the conflicts. Decide, what to keep and what to discard. VSCode has a built-in merge tool that can help you resolve the conflicts.

